      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63302,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fallopian tube</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:36:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63303,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Placental site nodule occurring in the fallopian tube is an incidental finding, usually weeks or even years after pregnancy {23564702; 8912838}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:36:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63304,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Placental site nodule occurring in the fallopian tube is very infrequent.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:36:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63305,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "<p>Placental site nodule occurring in the fallopian tube originates from remnants of an ectopic placental implantation site.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-01T14:58:43"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63306,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Clinicopathological and molecular studies demonstrate that placental site nodule/plaque most likely develops from the chorionic-type intermediate trophoblastic cells (associated with chorion laeve) rather than from the intermediate trophoblastic cells in the implantation sites.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:36:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63307,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "<p>The lesion presents as a nodule of fallopian tube.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-01T14:58:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63308,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Placental site nodule/plaque presents as a small discrete nodule or multiple lesions containing nests of trophoblastic cells embedded in abundant eosinophilic extracellular matrix (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/34/275\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Placental site nodule and plaque</em>). It is frequently associated with a relatively intense lymphocytic infiltrate surrounding the lesion. Immunohistochemistry using the markers specific to intermediate trophoblast (especially the chorionic-type), including HSD3B1 and p63, is useful in differential diagnosis. These markers are also positive in epithelioid trophoblastic tumour and other trophoblastic neoplasms.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-10T08:08:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63309,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 187,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-25T15:36:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
